# Ensemble Learning

Instead of depending on one model,

Ensemble Learning combines multiple models.

The idea is simple:

> Multiple good opinions are generally more reliable than one opinion.

Two major techniques are:

* Bagging
* Boosting

---

# Bagging

Bagging builds multiple models **in parallel**.

Every model gets a different bootstrap sample.

For Classification,

the final prediction is based on **Majority Voting**.

For Regression,

the final prediction is based on the **Mean**.

The main objective of Bagging is to reduce **Variance**.

---

# Bootstrapping

Bootstrapping randomly samples **rows (data points)** from the dataset **with replacement**.

Because sampling is with replacement,

some rows may appear multiple times,

while some rows may not appear at all.

Every tree still sees all the features,

but different subsets of the training rows.

---

# Random Forest

Random Forest is simply Bagging with an additional level of randomness.

Not only are the rows sampled randomly,

but each split also considers only a **random subset of features**.

Initially, I wondered why we would ignore some features.

The intuition is:

If one feature is always very dominant,

every Decision Tree will keep selecting that feature.

Then all the trees become similar.

Random Forest forces different trees to consider different features,

allowing them to learn different patterns.

This increases diversity and improves generalization.

---

# Boosting

Unlike Bagging,

Boosting trains models **sequentially**.

Every new model learns from the mistakes made by the previous one.

Its main objective is to reduce **Bias**.

---

# AdaBoost

AdaBoost focuses more on the samples that were previously classified incorrectly.

Misclassified samples receive higher importance.

The next weak learner pays more attention to those difficult examples.

Eventually,

many weak learners combine to form a strong learner.

---

# Gradient Boosting

Gradient Boosting is slightly different.

Instead of increasing sample weights,

it focuses on reducing the remaining error (residuals).

Each new tree tries to correct the mistakes made by the previous tree.

The objective is simple:

> Keep reducing the error step by step.

---

# XGBoost

XGBoost is an optimized implementation of Gradient Boosting.

It includes:

* Better optimization
* Regularization
* Automatic handling of missing values
* Faster training

This is why it performs extremely well on structured/tabular datasets and is widely used in industry.
